              {
                "id": "studio-brain-weekly-13",
                "type": "text",
                "values": {
                  "anchor": "",
                  "displayCondition": null,
                  "selectable": true,
                  "draggable": true,
                  "duplicatable": true,
                  "deletable": true,
                  "hideable": true,
                  "_meta": {
                    "htmlID": "u_content_text_4",
                    "htmlClassNames": "u_content_text"
                  },
                  "linkStyle": {
                    "inherit": true,
                    "linkColor": "#514293",
                    "linkHoverColor": "#514293",
                    "linkUnderline": true,
                    "linkHoverUnderline": true
                  },
                  "text": "Modern life is full of rewarding distractions: social media, streaming, snacks and endless notifications. When these become a big part of your day, other things can start to feel dull by comparison.<br><br>Enter the “brain reset”: the idea that stepping away from these for a while can restore your focus.<br><br>It’s a nice idea. The problem? That’s not quite how your brain works.<br><br><strong>First, what’s actually going on?</strong><br><br>The chemical people blame is often called the “pleasure chemical”, but it’s more closely linked to:<br><br>•&nbsp; Motivation<br>•&nbsp; Learning<br>•&nbsp; Noticing rewards<br>•&nbsp; Attention<br><br><strong>Can you actually reset it?</strong><br><br>Not really. Your brain needs it to work. What does change is what your brain <strong>learns to expect</strong>, which is why slow, deep activities can start to feel less engaging.<br><br><strong>So what should you do instead?</strong><br><br>Rather than a short “detox”, researchers suggest being more deliberate about where your attention goes over time:<br><br>•&nbsp; Turn off notifications you don’t need<br>•&nbsp; Make phone-free time in your day<br>•&nbsp; Spend more time on slower things that pay off<br>•&nbsp; Build habits around movement, people and hobbies<br><br><strong>The takeaway:</strong> resets make good social media posts, but they’re built on a misunderstanding. If you feel overstimulated, changing your daily habits will do more than a weekend off.",
                  "fontSize": "13px",
                  "fontFamily": {
                    "label": "Helvetica",
                    "value": "helvetica,sans-serif"
                  },
                  "color": "#1A1A1A",
                  "textAlign": "left",
                  "lineHeight": "160%",
                  "containerPadding": "16px 32px 8px"
                }
              },
